body:
I’d like to propose an opt-in follow-up to #31058 rather than duplicate its capacity-retry implementation.

The additional behavior would be a configurable fallback model used only for the remainder of the current agent turn when the selected model returns a structured capacity error.

Proposed recovery modes:

- preserve the existing/default terminal behavior where required;
- retry the selected model using the capacity policy proposed by #31058, once merged;
- after a configured number of primary retries—or immediately when configured—switch once to an explicitly selected fallback model for the rest of that turn.

Suggested fallback semantics:

- react only to structured `server_is_overloaded` / `slow_down` capacity errors;
- initially support only a fallback under the same provider/account;
- keep the user’s selected primary model unchanged;
- use the fallback for all remaining sampling steps in the current turn, including steps after tool calls;
- return to the selected primary on the next user turn;
- emit an explicit model-rerouted notice;
- allow only one fallback transition and no fallback chains;
- if the fallback is also overloaded, make no further model switches and defer to the shared capacity-exhaustion policy (currently terminal for the turn in #31058, potentially resumable once retained-state recovery is implemented);
- switch only at a safe sampling boundary, without replaying completed output, commands, tool calls, or other side effects;
- validate context-window, reasoning, modality, and tool compatibility before switching.

The retained-state pause/resume behavior requested in #22390 remains separate work; this fallback proposal would not by itself resolve that requirement.

I would intentionally exclude switching back to the primary during the same turn. Codex has no passive model-availability signal, so this would require speculative inference probes and model-switch hysteresis.

Would maintainers prefer this follow-up to remain under #22390 or be tracked as a separate enhancement? If the approach aligns with the roadmap, I would be happy to implement a focused PR after #31058 lands, but I will wait for an explicit invitation before writing code.

I investigated this against current `main` and built a local, integration-tested prototype that refreshes and extends the approach in #31058. Thanks @steipete-oai for establishing the capacity-specific retry direction there.

The smallest complete ownership boundary appears to be:

- classify only structured `server_is_overloaded` / `slow_down` responses at the provider boundary (no matching of the user-visible message);
- defer those failures out of the ordinary HTTP retry loop, then use a separate cancellation-aware patient budget in the turn loop (3 retries at 30s, 120s, and 300s, with positive-only jitter);
- reuse the same turn and client session so accepted input is recorded once and completed tool outputs are carried forward rather than replayed;
- intercept capacity before WebSocket-to-HTTP fallback, and never change the selected model implicitly;
- surface retry and terminal events as structured `ServerOverloaded`;
- after exhaustion, keep dependent Tab-queued input paused instead of dispatching it as though the failed turn completed (#33853).

I also traced the same error through local, remote-v1, and remote-v2 compaction (#35911). Pre-turn compaction is the important edge case: it runs before normal input recording, so terminal capacity exhaustion must preserve the already accepted input for the next user-initiated resume, exactly once. Previous-model compaction must also retain its model choice on capacity rather than silently falling back to the current model.

Because the complete sampling + compaction coverage is larger than the repository's 800-line review guideline, I propose two owner-aligned changes:

1. sampling/provider-boundary patient retry plus terminal dependent-queue safety;
2. compaction retry/cancellation plus pre-turn input resumption, based on the merged first change.

The prototype has focused integration coverage for independent HTTP/stream/capacity budgets, same-turn input and completed-tool preservation, cancellation during backoff, no capacity-triggered transport fallback, all three compaction paths, previous-model behavior, terminal observability, resumability, and queue safety.

The contribution policy requires an explicit maintainer invitation before opening an external PR. Would a maintainer please confirm whether I may submit stage 1 with this scope, and whether the two-stage split is the preferred review shape? I will not open a duplicate issue or unsolicited PR.
